last week's indictments against more  than a dozen Russian individuals and  organizations are increasing concerns  about the safety and integrity of this  fall's midterm elections  tonight correspondent Peter Doocy looks  at some of the biggest vulnerabilities  and what is being done about them to  figure out how to stop Moscow from  meddling in the midterms dozens of  secretaries of state spent a long  weekend in Washington for meetings that  started with a peek at Putin's playbook  in the latest Muller indictment that was  a million dollar a month operation so  that gives me an idea of the resources  that were working against most of that  money went towards propaganda the  biggest threat seems to be from the  American public believing the many false  stories that were told out there during  the last election but voting systems are  vulnerable to 21 states were targeted by  Russian hackers in 2016 and many  networks are still flawed the endpoint  security on most of those electoral  systems is outdated and it can't match  up to the modern-day weaponry being  created by the Russian cyber adversaries  during the 2016 cycle the FBI felt the  system was safe because it is so spread  out the beauty of the American voting  system is that it is dispersed among the  50 states and it's clunky as heck it's  not exactly a swift part of the internet  of things but things have changed now  that more precincts have Wi-Fi what's  happened is as the states have evolved  with their use of technology to  facilitate voting to increase the number  of voters to improve the process of  voting they've expanded the attack  surface that can now be penetrated by  foreign adversaries this weekend to  classified briefings were held for  election officials from every state the  Office of the Director of National  Intelligence says the sessions were all  about quote increasing awareness of  foreign adversary intent and  capabilities against the state's  election infrastructure as well as a  discussion of threat mitigation efforts  a DHS official tells FOX News that right  now 32 states and 31 local governments  are receiving remotely operated cyber  hygiene scans for vulnerabilities and 14  states have requested on-site risk and  vulnerability assessments which won't be  done till mid-april  but the DHS secretary Kirsten Nielsen is  telling state officials it still  ultimately up to  to keep elections secure she has  absolutely no desire or no intention to  take over elections and to run elections  from the federal level down to the state  level that was the thing we were the  most enthusiastic about hearing from  secretary Nielsen the first primaries of  the 2018 cycle are two weeks from  tomorrow so there is a great sense of  urgency to beef up cyber defenses even  though there is still no publicly  available evidence to show that Russia  or anybody ever hacked a polling place  in 2016 to change a ballot John why they  got a big task ahead of them Peter no  question about that
 

